  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Y?$\n\nI. $(Z∧Y)∨Y$\n\nII. $Y∧(Z∧Y)$\n\nIII. $Y∨(Y∨Z)$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nY∨(Y∧Z)≡Y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $Y.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Z∧Y)∨Y & ≡Y∨(Z∧Y) \\\\  & ≡Y∨(Y∧Z) \\\\  & ≡Y.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $Y.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $Y.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
